---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Oil Rig Welding — SMAW, TIG & FCAW Processes Explained"
  description="What welders actually do on a drilling rig: the SMAW, TIG and FCAW processes, where each is used, the four jobs rig welding splits into, and the conditions the work is done in."
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Welding', href: '/welding' }]}
  lang="en"
  alternateHref="/es/welding"
>
  <Nav slot="nav" lang="en" alternateHref="/es/welding" />
  <Footer slot="footer" lang="en" />

  <div class="page-hero">
    <div class="page-hero-eyebrow">09 — Trades</div>
    <h1>Rig <span>Welding</span></h1>
    <p class="page-hero-sub">Welding is one of the most critical trades on any oil rig. Structural failures in an offshore environment can be catastrophic — every weld matters.</p>
  </div>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="section-eyebrow">Processes</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Welding <span>Processes Used on Rigs</span></h2>

    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Process</th><th>Full Name</th><th>Best Used For</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>SMAW</td><td>Shielded Metal Arc (Stick)</td><td>Structural repairs, pipe, windy conditions</td><td>Workhorse of rig welding. Works outdoors. Simple equipment.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>GTAW</td><td>Gas Tungsten Arc (TIG)</td><td>Stainless steel, exotic alloys, precision</td><td>Highest quality weld. Slow. Requires clean conditions.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>FCAW</td><td>Flux-Cored Arc Welding</td><td>Fast structural welds, windy outdoor work</td><td>Self-shielded variant works in wind where MIG fails.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>GMAW</td><td>Gas Metal Arc (MIG)</td><td>Shop fabrication, indoor repairs</td><td>Fast and clean, but shielding gas disrupted by wind.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Hyperbaric</td><td>Dry Underwater Welding</td><td>Subsea structural repair</td><td>Done inside pressurized dry habitat. Best quality underwater weld, down to ~1,000m. See <a href="/welding/underwater-welding" style="color:var(--amber);">full guide</a>.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Wet</td><td>Wet Underwater Welding</td><td>Emergency repairs, 30–400 ft depth</td><td>Welder is directly in water. Lower quality than hyperbaric.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="two-col">
      <div>
        <h2 class="section-title" style="font-size:28px;">Which Standard <span>Applies to What</span></h2>
        <p style="color:var(--fog);font-size:13px;margin-bottom:16px;max-width:520px;">Rig welding is not governed by one standard. Which one applies depends on what you are welding, not who you work for — and a ticket to one does not qualify you for another.</p>
        <div class="card-grid grid-2">
          <div class="card">
            <h3 style="font-size:14px;">API 1104</h3>
            <p>Pipeline welding standard. Mandatory for most rig pipeline positions. Tests welding in multiple positions.</p>
            <span class="card-tag">Pipeline — Essential</span>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <h3 style="font-size:14px;">AWS D1.1</h3>
            <p>Structural steel welding standard. Required for platform, derrick, and structural work. 6G pipe is the highest standard.</p>
            <span class="card-tag">Structural — Essential</span>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <h3 style="font-size:14px;">AWS D3.6M</h3>
            <p>Underwater welding certification. Required for any subsea welding work.</p>
            <span class="card-tag">Underwater Welding</span>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <h3 style="font-size:14px;">ASME Section IX</h3>
            <p>Pressure vessel and boiler welding qualification. Required for high-pressure vessel and piping work.</p>
            <span class="card-tag">Pressure Vessels</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <a href="/welding/certifications" class="card-link" style="display:inline-block;margin-top:16px;">Full Certification Guide & Career Path →</a>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="section-title" style="font-size:28px;">Explore <span>Welding</span></h2>
        <div class="card-grid" style="grid-template-columns:1fr;">
          <div class="card">
            <span class="card-icon">🌊</span>
            <h3>Underwater Welding</h3>
            <p>How hyperbaric and wet welding actually work, what it pays, and how to become a commercial diver-welder.</p>
            {/* Named rather than "Read More". This is the highest-demand page
                on the site and every inbound link to it used a generic anchor,
                so nothing pointing at it said what it was about. */}
            <a href="/welding/underwater-welding" class="card-link">Hyperbaric &amp; Wet Welding →</a>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <span class="card-icon">📜</span>
            <h3>Certifications & Career Path</h3>
            <p>The complete progression from junior welder to CWI inspector, with pay at every stage.</p>
            <a href="/welding/certifications" class="card-link">Read More →</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="section-eyebrow">The Work</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Four Different <span>Jobs</span></h2>
    <p style="color:var(--fog);max-width:720px;margin-bottom:24px;">"Rig welder" covers four fairly different jobs. They use overlapping processes but different standards, different hours, and in practice different people — a hand who is excellent at one is not automatically employable in another.</p>

    <div class="card-grid grid-2">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Maintenance and Repair</h3>
        <p>The day-to-day: handrails, walkways, skid frames, cracked brackets, worn wear plates. Usually SMAW, usually awkward positions, often around live operations with a hot work permit in force. This is where most rig welders spend most of their time.</p>
        <span class="card-tag">Most Common</span>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Structural</h3>
        <p>Derrick members, substructure, crane pedestals, platform steel. Governed by AWS D1.1, inspected properly, and the work most likely to be scrutinised after the fact — because a failure here does not stay local.</p>
        <span class="card-tag">AWS D1.1</span>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Pipework and Pressure</h3>
        <p>Mud lines, choke and kill lines, pressure piping, vessels. API 1104 or ASME Section IX depending on the system. Root pass quality matters more than appearance, and radiography will find what a grinder hides.</p>
        <span class="card-tag">API 1104 / ASME IX</span>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Subsea</h3>
        <p>A different trade that happens to involve welding. You are a commercial diver first and a welder second, working to AWS D3.6M. Covered separately on the <a href="/welding/underwater-welding" style="color:var(--amber);">underwater welding page</a>.</p>
        <span class="card-tag">AWS D3.6M</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="note-box mt-24" style="max-width:760px;">
      <strong>Position matters more than process.</strong> Anyone can run a flat bead on a bench. What gets you hired is welding overhead, in the vertical, and around the back of a pipe you cannot see — which is why qualification tests are defined by position (1G through 6G) rather than by how good the weld looks in a photograph. The <a href="/welding/certifications" style="color:var(--amber);">certification guide</a> breaks the positions down.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="section-eyebrow">Conditions</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">What the Job Is <span>Actually Like</span></h2>
    <div class="two-col">
      <div>
        <p style="color:var(--mist);line-height:1.8;font-size:15px;">Rig welding is outdoor work on a steel structure that moves. Offshore, the deck is never entirely still, and wind pulls the shielding gas off a TIG puddle badly enough that a lot of subsea and topside work reverts to stick for that reason alone.</p>
        <p style="color:var(--mist);line-height:1.8;font-size:15px;">Almost every job requires a hot work permit, a fire watch, and a gas test before an arc is struck — the whole system is described in the <a href="/blog/permit-to-work-system" style="color:var(--amber);">permit to work guide</a>. On a live rig, the permit is often the constraint on when you can work, not the welding itself.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p style="color:var(--mist);line-height:1.8;font-size:15px;">The hazards are the usual ones concentrated into a small space: arc flash, hexavalent chromium in stainless fume, confined space entry, hot work next to hydrocarbon systems, and working at height on the derrick. Respiratory protection and the correct shade are not optional, and are covered in the <a href="/blog/rig-floor-ppe-guide" style="color:var(--amber);">PPE guide</a>.</p>
        <p style="color:var(--mist);line-height:1.8;font-size:15px;">Schedules follow the rig, not the trade — offshore rotations rather than day shifts. Pay reflects that, and is broken down by role in the <a href="/careers/salary" style="color:var(--amber);">salary guide</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
